## What the engine run must confirm, and what to do if it does not

These are the only constructs in the theme that a static check cannot prove. Each has a stated remedy; none of them is a design decision.

1. `fallbacks = Array[Font]([SubResource("SystemFont_Fallback")])` on the role fonts. If the loader rejects the typed-array form, delete the `fallbacks` lines and record it — the claim they support (system fallback for symbols/emoji, UIR-01 §1) then moves to whoever ports those glyphs.
2. `SystemFont` with `font_names = PackedStringArray("system-ui", ...)`. If macOS resolves none of the names, the engine falls back to its default UI font; that is the intended `system-ui` behaviour, so a warning line is not a failure.
3. `spacing_glyph` (integer px) on the role fonts — the rounding table in the theme README §1 is the record; nothing to fix unless a screen looks wrong, which is GATE-A's call.
4. The probe's height bands (primary button 40-62 px, segmented chip 34-52 px) are the one measurement-derived assertion in the file; they were set around the browser's 51 px and 42 px. If the engine reports a value outside the band, that is a real finding to report, not a threshold to widen.

